In a time when women  had very few options in life 
Maria Ga\"{e}tana Agnesi (1718-1799) was both exceptional and very
lucky.
Her brilliance
and talent were recognized early and nurtured by her wealthy
father. He provided her with the best possible tutors to develop her
talents. She mastered the Calculus of Newton and Leibniz and in $1748$
wrote a series of textbooks on the topic titled \emph{Instituzioni Analitiche ad
  Uso Della Giovent\`u{} Italiana\footnote{Translation: Analytical
    Methods for the use of Italian Youth}.} It was immediately
recognized as a masterpiece of exposition, was used throughout
Europe during eighteenth century, and was by far the most popular
Calculus textbook in use at the time.
The Englishman, John Colson, was so impressed with Agnesi's work that  in 1760 
he took it upon himself to learn Italian specifically so that
he could translate her text into English\footnote{Maria Agnesi was
  remarkable woman and well worth learning more about.  If you are
  interested you can find more at
  {\tt{}http://www-history.mcs.st-and.ac.uk/Biographies/Agnesi.html}.}.
Since her text is intended  for teaching it contains none
of Agnesi's original mathematics. Instead she collected many of the
known results of the time and organized them for the instruction of
students. The Latin name of one of the curves that she used for
instruction was \emph{versoria} (``rope that turns a sail'') because
of its shape. Agnesi translated this, correctly, into the Italian as
``la versiera.''  Unfortunately Colson mistook this for
``l'aversiera'' which means ``witch.'' As a result this particular
curve has been known ever since as the \underline{Witch of Agnesi}.


