Whenever you encounter a new curve an important question is, ``What is
its derivative?'' In this case this is an easy question to answer.

\begin{embeddedproblem}{}
  Show that if $y=\frac{1}{1+x^2},$ then
  $\dfdx{y}{x}=\frac{-2x}{(1+x^2)^2}.$
\end{embeddedproblem}

Computing the derivative as a \emph{formula} is very useful. It has,
in fact, been our primary focus so far in this text. However to truly
understand the relationship between a function and its  derivative
nothing can replace seeing both of them graphed together:\\
\centerline{\includegraphics*[height=2in,width=4in]{../Figures/Witch3}}
Look closely at the relationship between the Witch (in red) and its
derivative (in blue). Is it clear how these graphs are related?

Notice in particular that when $x=0$ the $y$ coordinate of the Witch
is at its highest point (maximum value), whereas the $y$ coordinate of its
\underline{derivative} is zero. Does this make sense?

Of course it does. We know that the derivative of a curve at a given
point gives us the slope of the tangent line at that point. Wherever
the Witch achieves a maximum the tangent line will clearly be
horizontal. That is, the slope will be zero. That is exactly what these
graphs tell us: When the Witch (red curve) is at its maximum, its
slope is zero. Thus the derivative passes through the point $(0,0).$
Similarly, when $x$ is just to the of right of zero, the slope of the
Witch is small, but negative, and just to the left of zero the slope
of the Witch is small, but positive. At the extreme right end of the
graph above the slope of the Witch is small, but negative and at the
extreme left it is small, but positive. And all of this is reflected
in the shape of the blue (derivative) curve.

It is a little more interesting to reverse this process. That is,
suppose we could see only the derivative of the Witch (blue
curve). Could we figure out the shape of the Witch from this?

Sure we can. Well, almost.

Consider: From the extreme left of the graph to $x=0$ the
$y-$coordinate of the derivative curve is above the $x-$axis. That is,
the slope of the Witch is positive. Clearly wherever the slope of a
function is positive the function is increasing, so we can deduce that
the Witch increases from left to right until we reach $x=0.$

Thereafter the $y-$coordinate of the derivative curve is below the
$x-$axis. That is, the slope of the Witch is negative, so the Witch is
decreasing.

So, just by looking at the derivative curve, we can see that the Witch
of Agnesi increases from left to right until we reach $x=0,$ and after
that it decreases. Unfortunately, this is not enough to completely
describe the Witch of Agnesi.  The fact is that we can tell a lot
about a curve by looking at its derivative, but we can't tell
everything.

