\subsection*{The Differential of $\inverse{\tan}(x)$
    and $\inverse\cot(x).$}
\label{sec:diff-invers-invers}

Reasoning from pictures as we've done can give strong insights into
many problems and we encourage you do do it whenever you can.
However, the most we can hope for in reasoning from pictures is an
intuitive feel for the general shape of the curves involved. We now
need to confirm this intuition analytically.

To help with the analytic aspect, note that the name \emph{arctangent}
literally means ``the angle (arc) whose tangent  is\footnote{It actually
  helps to read it this way too.}''.
More precisely
$$
y=\arctan(x)\text{ exactly when } \tan(y)=x.
$$

Differentiating to find $\dx{y}$ gives:
\begin{align*}
  \dx(\tan(y)) &= \dx{x}\\
  \sec^2(y)\dx{y} &= \dx{x}\\
  \dx{y}&= \frac{1}{\sec^2(y)}\dx{x}.
\end{align*}
\begin{wrapfigure}[]{r}{1.2in}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\centerline{\includegraphics*[height=1in,width=1.2in]{../Figures/arctanMnemonic2}}
\label{fig:arctanMnemonic2}
\end{wrapfigure}
To convert this to an expression in $x$, we remember that $y$ is the
angle whose tangent is $x$ and use the visual aid at the right.
Since $\text{secant } = \frac{\text{hypotenuse}}{\text{adjacent}},$ we
have $\sec(y)= \frac{\sqrt{1+x^2}}{1}=\sqrt{1+x^2}$, so that
$$
\dx{(\arctan(y))}=\dx{y}=\frac{1}{\sec^2(y)}\dx{x}=\frac{1}{1+x^2}\dx{y}.
$$

This shows that our intuition about the Witch was correct. A similar
derivation can be used for the arccotangent.

