As was said in the problem, this is weird.   It is even stranger if we
try to graph something like $y=\arctan\left(\frac{1+x}{1-x}\right)$
and compare it to the graph of
$y=\arctan(x)+\arctan(1)=\arctan(x)+\frac{\pi}{4}$.\\
\centerline{\includegraphics*[height=2in,width=5in]{../Figures/arctanabs1}}

First notice that this function really doesn't exist for $x=1$, but it
has a jump as well.  What happened?  Actually, the software package
did not graph $y=\arctan\left(\frac{1+x}{1-x}\right)$, but actually graphed $y=\inverse\tan\left(\frac{1+x}{1-x}\right)$.  So, what is the difference?
This brings up what we actually mean by a function.  In truth,
$y=y(x)=\arctan(x)$ is not
really a function.  To be a function, then for every $y$ value, there
must be a single value that we can call $y(x)$.  A graph of
$y=\arctan(x)$ is below.\\
\centerline{\includegraphics*[height=2in,width=4in]{../Figures/arctan3}}

Notice it is not a function.  This is because $x=\tan(y)=\tan(x+n\pi)$for any integer $n$.  We
say that arctangent is actually a multifunction and each of the
branches are surprisingly called ``branches.''   When the software
package was evaluating $\arctan\left(\frac{1+x}{1-x}\right)$, it was looking for the angle $y$ on the branch $\frac{-\pi}{2}<y<\frac{\pi}{2}$.
This is called the principal branch.  For $x<1$,
$\arctan\left(\frac{1+x}{1-x}\right)=\arctan(x)+\frac{\pi}{4}$.
However, for $x>1$,\notetoself{Something missing here  -- Aug 15, 2020} .  This
can be seen if we superimpose the graphs of
$\arctan\left(\frac{1+x}{1-x}\right)$  and the two branches of
$y=\arctan(x)+\frac{\pi}{4}$. \\
\centerline{\includegraphics*[height=1.5in,width=4in]{../Figures/arctan4}}

\begin{mynotation}{Inverse Function}
  It is easy to understand what the inverse of a function is: If the
  function $\tan$ takes $x$ to $y$ ($y=\tan(x)$), then the function
  $\inverse\tan$ ``undoes'' $\tan$ ($x=\inverse\tan(y)$).

  The concept really is that simple.

  But function inversion can be difficult to think about, partly
  because it is so abstract but also because the notation we use can
  be confusing. Probably the clearest notation we have is the ``arc''
  designation. The symbol ``$\arctan,$'' for example, clearly signals
  that the function, $\arctan(x)$ returns an angle (an ``arc'' of a
  circle).  In the computer age this has been shortened to
  ${\rm atan}$ for reasons that are no longer apparent.

  By far the worst notation  is the use of the ``$-1$'' as an exponent to
  indicate function inversion, as in $\inverse\tan(x)$. Unfortunately, this
 is also probably the most common, so it has to be dealt
  with.

  This is too
  easily confused with reciprocation as in: $2^{-1} = \frac12.$ It is
  {\large\bf{}\underline{not true}} that
  $\inverse{\tan}(x) = \frac{1}{\tan(x)}.$  An easy way to remember what $\inverse{\tan}(x)$ means is to
  read the symbol $\inverse{\tan}(x)$ as ``the angle whose tangent is
  $x.$'' This emphasizes that $x$ is the tangent of some angle, and
  $\inverse{\tan}(x)$ is that angle.

  The ``$-1$'' notation comes from the fact that both are inverses, just
  different kinds: $\inverse2$ is the \emph{multiplicative} inverse of
  $2$ while $y=\inverse\tan(x)$ is the \emph{functional} inverse of
  $x=\tan(y)$. In the former case the ``$-1$'' in the exponent indicates
  an -- take the reciprocal of $2.$ In the latter the the ``$-1$'' is part
  of the name of the function. In fact $\inverse{\tan}$ and $\Arctan$
  are different names of the same function. You can think of the ``$-1$''
  as an abbreviation of ``Arc.'' Read them both as ``the principle
  angle whose tangent is''.

  Tthe other five inverse trigonometric functions, $\inverse\sin,$
  $\inverse\cos,$ $\inverse\cot,$ $\inverse\sec,$ and $\inverse\csc$,
  have similiar issues. We consider them next.

\end{mynotation}

Thus we have
$$
\dx{(\inverse\tan(x))}=\frac{1}{1+x^2}\dx{x}.
$$
Given the fact that $\arccot(x)=\frac{\pi}{2}-\arctan(x)$, we can
define the principal branch of that as well to obtain the inverse
function $\inverse\cot(x)=\frac{\pi}{2}-\inverse\tan(x)$.  Since
$\frac{-\pi}{2}<\inverse\tan(x)<\frac{\pi}{2}$, then
$0<\inverse\cot(x)<\pi$ and this represents the principal value.  We
still have
$$
\dx{(\inverse\cot(x))}=\frac{-1}{1+x^2}\dx{x}.
$$



