Whereas the graphs of $y=\arctan(x)$ and $y=\arccot(x)$ lend
themselves to identifying a principal branch and principal value, it
is a little trickier for the other inverse trigonometric functions.
Take, for example, the graph of $y=\arcsin(x)$.  For a given value of
$x$ between $-1$ and $1$ ,
there are infinitely many values for $y=\arcsin(x)$.

Unlike the case of the arctangent and arccotangent, finding the
appropriate principal value of the arcsin actually affects our
derivative.  To see this, let's differentiate arcsine and see what
happens.  If $y=\arcsin(x)$, then $\sin(y)=x$, and
\begin{align*}
  \d{(\sin(x))}&= \dx{x}\\
  \cos(y)&=\dx{x}\\
  \dx{y}&=\frac{1}{\cos(y)}\dx{x}.
\end{align*}
So far, so good.  The issue comes from converting this back into an
expression in $x$.  Let's go back to the unit circle and see what
happens.\\
\centerline{\includegraphics*[height=2in,width=2.5in]{../Figures/arcsin3}}
Again, by the Pythagorean Theorem, $\cos^2(y)=1-\sin^2(y)$.  However,
this gives $\cos(y)=\pm\sqrt{1-\sin^2(y)} = \pm \sqrt{1-x^2}$.  Thus,
$$
\dx{(\arcsin(x))}=\frac{\pm1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\dx{x}
$$
with the sign (no pun intended) of this depending on what quadrant the angle $y$ is in.
