  Again, finding the principal value for the last two remaining
  inverse trigonometric functions is more complicated than
  differentiating.  In fact, there is not even universal agreement
  among mathematicians what the principal branch should be.  Let's
  start by differentiating $y=\arcsec(x)$.  This is pretty
  straightforward.
  \begin{align*}
    dx{(\sec(y))}&=\dx{x}\\
    \sec(y)\tan(y)\dx{y}&= \dx{x}\\
    \dx{y}&=\frac{{1}}{\sec(y)\tan(y)}\dx{x}.\\
    \intertext{Nothing really surprising here. Furthermore
    $\sec(y)=x$, so we get}
    \dx{(\arcsec(x))}&= \frac{1}{x\tan(y)}\dx.
  \end{align*}
